WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.176
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:08.816
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.116 --> 00:00:15.832
Unit twenty-five: Visualising Data. Today you will add emphasis with inversion after 'Not only'.

4
00:00:16.132 --> 00:00:18.912
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:23.227
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:25.317
Visualisation.

7
00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:29.524
Visualisation means the graphical representation of data.

8
00:00:30.117 --> 00:00:32.824
Good visualisation reveals patterns instantly.

9
00:00:33.417 --> 00:00:36.777
Your turn. Say: Visualisation.

10
00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:40.925
Visualisation.

11
00:00:41.683 --> 00:00:42.429
Chart.

12
00:00:43.417 --> 00:00:47.715
Chart means a diagram displaying data, such as a bar or line chart.

13
00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:50.850
A line chart suits data over time.

14
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.905
Your turn. Say: Chart.

15
00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:57.547
Chart.

16
00:00:58.283 --> 00:00:59.366
Axis.

17
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:03.615
Axis means a reference line along which values are measured.

18
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:06.472
Always label both axes clearly.

19
00:01:07.083 --> 00:01:09.422
Your turn. Say: Axis.

20
00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:13.314
Axis.

21
00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:14.807
Trend.

22
00:01:15.817 --> 00:01:18.861
Trend means a general direction of change over time.

23
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.879
The trend reversed after 2020.

24
00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:25.170
Your turn. Say: Trend.

25
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:28.814
Trend.

26
00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:30.447
Outlier.

27
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.599
Outlier means a value far removed from the general pattern.

28
00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:37.926
The outlier was traced to a data entry error.

29
00:01:38.517 --> 00:01:41.030
Your turn. Say: Outlier.

30
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:44.856
Outlier.

31
00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:46.769
Misleading.

32
00:01:47.783 --> 00:01:51.373
Misleading means giving a false impression, often deliberately.

33
00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:54.645
A truncated axis can be highly misleading.

34
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:58.088
Your turn. Say: Misleading.

35
00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:01.981
Misleading.

36
00:02:02.717 --> 00:02:03.648
Scale.

37
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:08.519
Scale means the range and intervals of values on an axis.

38
00:02:09.117 --> 00:02:12.150
A logarithmic scale compresses large differences.

39
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.077
Your turn. Say: Scale.

40
00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:18.842
Scale.

41
00:02:19.583 --> 00:02:20.550
Insight.

42
00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:25.256
Insight means a deep, accurate understanding gained from analysis.

43
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:28.523
The map provided an unexpected insight.

44
00:02:29.117 --> 00:02:31.792
Your turn. Say: Insight.

45
00:02:34.633 --> 00:02:35.739
Insight.

46
00:02:36.533 --> 00:02:40.184
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:44.582
Visal, why did you redesign the chart in your report?

48
00:02:45.683 --> 00:02:52.074
The original had a truncated axis. A tiny increase looked enormous, which was misleading.

49
00:02:53.183 --> 00:02:55.438
Does design really matter that much?

50
00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:03.939
Absolutely. Not only does a clear chart save time, but it also builds trust in your conclusions.

51
00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:06.887
What else should designers avoid?

52
00:03:07.983 --> 00:03:16.882
Clutter. Not only do 3D effects distort proportions, but they also distract from the actual insight.

53
00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:19.998
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:24.150
Grammar focus: Inversion for emphasis (Not only).

55
00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:25.788
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:26.483 --> 00:03:30.236
Not only does a clear chart save time, but it also builds trust.

57
00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:36.307
Not only do 3D effects distort proportions, but they also distract readers.

58
00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:41.039
Not only was the axis truncated, but the labels were also missing.

59
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:51.883
After a negative adverbial like 'Not only' at the start, invert the subject and auxiliary as in a question: 'Not only does it …', 'Not only was it …'. This is formal and emphatic.

60
00:03:52.617 --> 00:03:57.301
Be careful! Don't say: Not only a chart saves time, but builds trust.

61
00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:03.101
Say: Not only does a chart save time, but it also builds trust.

62
00:04:03.401 --> 00:04:06.909
Invert after 'Not only': does + subject + verb.

63
00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.654
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:12.793
Seeing Is Believing.

65
00:04:13.093 --> 00:04:18.320
Data visualisation transforms rows of numbers into patterns the human eye can grasp instantly.

66
00:04:19.333 --> 00:04:24.317
Not only does a clear chart save time, but it also builds trust in the analysis behind it.

67
00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:31.978
Yet visualisation can deceive as easily as it informs, particularly when an axis is truncated or a scale distorted.

68
00:04:32.967 --> 00:04:38.739
Not only do such techniques exaggerate trends, but they may also conceal important outliers.

69
00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:45.901
Responsible designers therefore prioritise accuracy over visual drama, letting the data speak honestly.

70
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.153
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:52.017 --> 00:04:56.907
Not only blank a clear chart save time, but it also builds trust.

72
00:04:57.207 --> 00:05:00.280
A, do. B, does. C, is.

73
00:05:03.833 --> 00:05:06.146
The answer is B: does.

74
00:05:07.283 --> 00:05:10.897
Correct the inversion: 'Not only the axis was truncated …'

75
00:05:11.197 --> 00:05:18.688
A, Not only was the axis truncated …. B, Not only the axis truncated was …. C, Not only did the axis was truncated ….

76
00:05:22.233 --> 00:05:25.464
The answer is A: Not only was the axis truncated ….

77
00:05:26.617 --> 00:05:31.008
A bar chart whose axis starts at 95 instead of 0 may be blank.

78
00:05:31.308 --> 00:05:35.286
A, insightful. B, misleading. C, logarithmic.

79
00:05:38.833 --> 00:05:40.879
The answer is B: misleading.

80
00:05:42.017 --> 00:05:44.875
A general direction of change over time is a blank.

81
00:05:45.175 --> 00:05:48.271
A, trend. B, scale. C, axis.

82
00:05:51.833 --> 00:05:53.519
The answer is A: trend.

83
00:05:54.683 --> 00:05:57.972
According to the reading, what can misleading techniques conceal?

84
00:05:58.272 --> 00:06:02.691
A, important outliers. B, the title. C, the colour scheme.

85
00:06:06.233 --> 00:06:09.057
The answer is A: important outliers.

86
00:06:10.267 --> 00:06:13.186
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:16.282
How can a chart mislead its readers?

88
00:06:33.083 --> 00:06:35.048
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:35.348 --> 00:07:13.930
A chart can mislead in several subtle ways. Not only can a truncated axis make a small change look dramatic, but a distorted scale can also hide important differences. For example, a bar chart starting at ninety-five rather than zero might suggest that one school vastly outperforms another when the gap is tiny. Some designers argue that zooming in helps readers see detail, which is sometimes fair. However, the context should always be made clear so that readers are not deceived.

90
00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:15.916
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:16.817 --> 00:07:21.617
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and inversion for emphasis (not only).

92
00:07:21.917 --> 00:07:26.090
Today's value is truthfulness. Tell the truth and check the facts.

93
00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:29.437
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:29.737 --> 00:07:33.249
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

